      "title": "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "65d2108b-4f68-5279-a4d6-96817df665e2",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Find $\\frac{df}{dx}$ when $f(x)=(- 12 x^{4} + 12 x)^{2}$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{2},\\,g=- 12 x^{4} + 12 x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=12 - 48 x^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=2g\\cdot (12 - 48 x^{3}) \\\\  &=2(- 12 x^{4} + 12 x)(12 - 48 x^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
